%I A401518 #16 Nov 16 2026 15:25:11
%S A401518 -1,-1,1,1,1,-1,-3,-3,-3,1,-1,3,-5,1,-5,3,-5,1,-1,-1,5,-7,7,-5,1,5,-1,
%T A401518 5,3,5,-1,-11,9,-5,-7,-7,-5,-13,-5,-7,-1,5,-5,-9,-11,7,3,3,1,-5,-17,7,
%U A401518 -5,-3,-1,3,-9,9,-21,7,-5,3,-9,-21,-7,13,-5,7,9,-9,-1
%N A401518 a(n) = t(p) = (A_1 - A_3)/p, where p = A007521(n) and A_j is the sum of the elements of the j-th coset of the fourth powers mod p, the quartic character being normalized by chi_4(2) = i.
%C A401518 Definition:
%C A401518 Write t(p) for the value at the prime p, so that a(n) = t(p) with p = A007521(n). For p == 5 (mod 8) the fourth powers H form a subgroup of index 4, and 2 is a quadratic nonresidue, so 2^((p-1)/4) mod p is a primitive fourth root of unity mod p. Requiring chi_4(2) = i therefore determines the quartic character, the four cosets are C_j = 2^j*H, and A_j is the sum of the elements of C_j. Chi_4(2) = i must be pinned, or the signs swap. (The companion invariant k(p) is the [quartic residue (A3) - quadratic non-quartic residue (A1)] normalized by dividing by p. This represents the real part of the classical formulas below. k(p)'s sign is canonical.)
%C A401518 Characteristics:
%C A401518 t(p) is always an integer, except where p = 5 [theorem], is always odd [theorem], and grows slowly, e.g. t(7950461) = 713 [empirical].
%C A401518 Applications (classical):
%C A401518 - k(p) + t(p)*i = B_{1,chi_4}, the first generalized Bernoulli number of chi_4, which is -L(0,chi_4). [theorem]
%C A401518 - k(p)^2 + t(p)^2 = 2*h^-(p), where h^-(p) = [h^-] is the relative class number of the imaginary cyclic quartic field of conductor p. [theorem: Hudson and Williams (1981); Setzer (1980), Proposition 2; Park and Kwon (1998), Section 4]
%D A401518 K. Girstmair, The relative class numbers of imaginary cyclic fields of degrees 4, 6, 8 and 10, Math. Comp. 61 (1993), 881-887.
%D A401518 K. Girstmair, Class number factors and distribution of residues, Abh. Math. Sem. Univ. Hamburg 67 (1997), 65-104.
%D A401518 R. H. Hudson, Class numbers of imaginary cyclic quartic fields and related quaternary systems, Pacific J. Math. 115 (1984), 129-142.
%D A401518 R. H. Hudson and K. S. Williams, A class number formula for certain quartic fields, Carleton Mathematical Series No. 174, Carleton University, Ottawa, 1981.
%D A401518 Y.-H. Park and S.-H. Kwon, Determination of all non-quadratic imaginary cyclic number fields of 2-power degree with relative class number <= 20, Acta Arith. 83 (1998), 211-223.
%D A401518 B. Setzer, The determination of all imaginary, quartic, abelian number fields with class number 1, Math. Comp. 35 (1980), 1383-1386.
%H A401518 Joseph Farland, <a href="/A401518/b401518.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 2..10000</a>
%H A401518 Haobo Ma, Mircea Merca, and Wenlin Zhang, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29608">Residue Sums Modulo Products of Two Distinct Odd Primes</a>, arXiv:2610.29608 [math.NT], 2026.
%F A401518 t(p) = (A_1 - A_3)/p = Im B_{1,chi_4}, with chi_4(2) = i.
%F A401518 t(p) = (1/(4*p))*Sum_{x=1..p-1} ((2*x^4 mod p) - (8*x^4 mod p)), since x -> x^4 mod p is four-to-one onto C_0, C_1 = 2*C_0 and C_3 = 8*C_0.
%F A401518 t(p)^2 = 2*h^-(p) - k(p)^2, with k(p) = A392984 and h^-(p) = [h^-].
%e A401518 p = 13 = A007521(2): the fourth powers are C_0 = {1, 3, 9}, so C_1 = 2*C_0 = {2, 6, 5} and C_3 = 8*C_0 = {8, 11, 7}. Then A_1 = 13, A_3 = 26 and t(13) = (13 - 26)/13 = -1, giving a(2) = -1.
%o A401518 (Python)
%o A401518 from sympy import primerange
%o A401518 def t(p):
%o A401518     e = (p - 1)//4
%o A401518     ip = pow(2, e, p)                      # chi_4(2) = i
%o A401518     A1 = sum(n for n in range(1, p) if pow(n, e, p) == ip)
%o A401518     A3 = sum(n for n in range(1, p) if pow(n, e, p) == pow(ip, 3, p))
%o A401518     return (A1 - A3)//p
%o A401518 print([t(p) for p in primerange(13, 3000) if p % 8 == 5])
%Y A401518 Cf. A007521, A392984 (the coordinate k).
%K A401518 sign,easy,new
%O A401518 2,7
